00:00:02.629 --> 00:00:04.610 align:start position:0%
rescue crews in Indonesia are racing to  find survivors after a devastating pair  of natural disasters killed about 850  people a powerful 7.5 magnitude  earthquake struck the island of Sulawesi  Friday had triggered a tsunami that  devastated coastal communities hundreds  of people are hurt or missing including  many who are believed to be buried in  mud in Pulu tens of thousands are  homeless Ben Tracy is in Indonesia and  traveling to the hardest hit areas good  morning the death toll here is still  expected to climb and that's because it  has been extremely difficult for  rescuers to get into some of these areas  that were hardest hit and where they  fear many people have died trapped by  rubble and surrounded by mud this woman  spent more than 48 hours waiting for  rescue one of over a million Indonesians  impacted by Friday's earthquake cell  phone video shows the quakes power as  the ground cracks and explodes the earth  rising and falling like waves the  seismic shock also triggered a tsunami  bringing a wall of water nearly 20 feet  high roaring over the island of Sulawesi  it came without warning after the  earthquake cut power to the tsunami  alert system the twin disasters  devastated this city of Palo where  crowded buildings like mosques and malls  collapsed trapping people inside until  Sunday night cries for help could be  heard from the rubble of the roarr roarr  hotel where officials believe 50 people  could be buried under the debris while  some crews are still racing to save  survivors others have turned to the grim  task of collecting bodies the disaster  has left nearly 50 thousand Indonesians  homeless and in care of the military  while surveying the devastation the  Indonesian president told survivors  recovery efforts will take time we  didn't expect it to be like this he said  so we hope and pray for the communities  and be patient a lack of heavy machinery  in these coastal areas is slowing  efforts to remove rubble and potentially  save lives meanwhile 1200 prisoners have  escaped from three different prisons  that were  rocked by this earthquake authorities  are so focused on the search-and-rescue  operation they don't have time to try to  track them down for CBS this morning  then tracy Makassar indonesia
 

